1
00:00:21,786 --> 00:00:25,840
The warplane was born at the start of World War I,

2
00:00:25,841 --> 00:00:29,260
when fragile British and German spotter aircraft

3
00:00:29,261 --> 00:00:32,520
first ventured over the trenches of the Western Front.

4
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,580
They were made of wood, wire, and canvas.

5
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,100
By the end of the war in 1918,

6
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:41,520
air battles involving bombers and fighters

7
00:00:41,521 --> 00:00:44,900
had become an established part of modern warfare.

8
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:50,220
By the 1930s, very little had changed.

9
00:00:50,221 --> 00:00:52,940
The aircraft were still biplanes.

10
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:54,920
They were still made of wood

11
00:00:54,921 --> 00:00:57,940
covered with fabric, and they were slow.

12
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:11,280
Then, in 1931, British seaplane reached the astonishing speed of 407 miles per hour,

13
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:15,920
more than twice as fast as the biplanes then in service with most air forces.

14
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:23,720
The S-6B was a streamlined metal monoplane far ahead of its time.

15
00:01:23,721 --> 00:01:29,940
It was the latest in a series of racing planes created by the Supermarine Aircraft Company.

16
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:34,660
Its chief designer, Reginald Mitchell, was a brilliant engineer

17
00:01:34,661 --> 00:01:39,000
who understood that the day of the traditional biplane was over.

18
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:43,380
In 1930, the British government officially requested

19
00:01:43,381 --> 00:01:47,400
that its aircraft industry submit designs for a new fighter.

20
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:52,280
Mitchell produced an aircraft which stuck closely to the government's request,

21
00:01:52,281 --> 00:01:56,160
but was little better than the biplanes it was meant to replace.

22
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:59,100
Mitchell went back to the drawing board.

23
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:06,020
Then, in 1933, a new sense of urgency developed.

24
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:09,800
The Nazi party was voted into power in Germany.

25
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:14,100
Its leader, Adolf Hitler, began rearming his country

26
00:02:14,101 --> 00:02:16,920
with modern all-metal bombers and fighters.

27
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:29,000
Mitchell was diagnosed with cancer and underwent a major operation.

28
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:34,460
While convalescing in Europe, he talked with German pilots

29
00:02:34,461 --> 00:02:39,780
who were enthusiastic about the new aircraft designs and itching for combat.

30
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:43,660
He returned home to England convinced that war was coming.

31
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:48,380
He was determined to match the best that Germany could produce.

32
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:58,900
Meanwhile, Britain's Rolls-Royce company was privately developing a new aircraft engine.

33
00:02:58,901 --> 00:03:03,500
It was powerful and more sophisticated than anything before it.

34
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:05,760
It was christened the Merlin.

35
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:13,960
Mitchell realized that this engine could give him the extra power he needed to create a high-speed fighter.

36
00:03:13,961 --> 00:03:21,960
In 1933, he began rough sketches for a new design based on his own ideas, not official

37
00:03:21,961 --> 00:03:22,960
specifications.

38
00:03:22,961 --> 00:03:30,260
It had a streamlined fuselage, enclosed cockpit, and thin section wings, which contained folding

39
00:03:30,261 --> 00:03:32,960
wheels and eight machine guns.

40
00:03:32,961 --> 00:03:37,960
Mitchell's health was failing rapidly, and he was in constant pain.

41
00:03:37,961 --> 00:03:42,960
But by January 1935, work started on the prototype.

42
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:57,960
In a moment of pure inspiration, Mitchell created the gracefully curved wing shape, which would become the hallmark of the Spitfire.

43
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:12,880
where biplanes had used a web of wires to brace their wooden frame.

44
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:18,620
Mitchell's machine used a stressed metal skin, riveted to metal forms.

45
00:04:18,621 --> 00:04:21,920
The wings were built around a laminated spar,

46
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:26,760
and its many layers combined great strength with flexibility.

47
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:32,000
It was a complex and brilliant design, but it would be a nightmare.

48
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:34,160
to mass produce.

49
00:04:34,161 --> 00:04:36,920
I think sometimes men with bright minds

50
00:04:36,921 --> 00:04:39,460
have flares of genius.

51
00:04:39,461 --> 00:04:42,860
I think this was a flare.

52
00:04:42,861 --> 00:04:45,460
I think he must have been mulling over in his mind

53
00:04:45,461 --> 00:04:48,260
how you can get away from the old by-play

54
00:04:48,261 --> 00:04:51,340
and still retain the stress factors for diving,

55
00:04:51,341 --> 00:04:54,440
the thrust of guns, the thrust of power, and everything else.

56
00:04:54,441 --> 00:04:55,540
Where are you going to put this?

57
00:04:55,541 --> 00:04:56,840
Are you going to put it in the wings?

58
00:04:56,841 --> 00:04:58,780
Are you going to put it in the fuselage?

59
00:04:58,781 --> 00:05:00,280
How are you going to do this?

60
00:05:00,281 --> 00:05:05,860
It was a very advanced form of construction in those days,

61
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:08,420
but you see, R.J. Mitchell was an exceptional man.

62
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:13,020
I can't think of anyone else that approached him.

63
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:16,680
By the spring of 1936,

64
00:05:16,681 --> 00:05:18,760
the prototype airframe

65
00:05:18,761 --> 00:05:21,500
and the new Rolls-Royce Merlin engine were ready.

66
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:23,400
The two were combined.

67
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:28,720
And on March 6th, the very first Spitfire,

68
00:05:28,721 --> 00:05:32,200
number K5054 took off.

69
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:35,720
It was a strikingly beautiful and revolutionary machine,

70
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:38,980
which looked more like a racer than a fighter.

71
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:42,940
After its initial flights,

72
00:05:43,340 --> 00:05:48,060
the prototype was flown by supermarine test pilot Jeffrey Quill.

73
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:51,700
The cockpit was narrow, but not cramped.

74
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:58,000
The instrument panel was tidy, symmetric, and logically laid out.

75
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:01,720
At once I felt good in that cockpit.

76
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:10,720
I primed the Merlin engine carefully and it started first time.

77
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:20,720
I did my cockpit checks.

78
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:24,720
With a last look round for other aircraft,

79
00:06:24,721 --> 00:06:27,720
I turned into wind and opened the throttle.

80
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:42,120
Soon we were airborne and climbing away.

81
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:47,040
Once fully airborne, the aircraft began to slip along as if on skates,

82
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:49,680
with an immediate impression of effortless performance.

83
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:53,740
Here, I thought to myself, is a real lady.

84
00:06:53,741 --> 00:06:59,040
The lady was fast, and she certainly had looks.

85
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:00,840
But was she a fighter?

86
00:07:03,940 --> 00:07:07,580
Rolls-Royce piston engines were named after birds of prey.

87
00:07:08,020 --> 00:07:09,620
Their jets were named after rivers.

88
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:12,500
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

89
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:22,660
The revolutionary Spitfire was not the only contestant in the race to build a new RAF fighter.

90
00:07:22,661 --> 00:07:27,340
The Hawker Hurricane was a much less advanced design than the Spitfire,

91
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:32,220
with a thick section wing and a wire-braced fuselage with a fabric covering.

92
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:34,220
But it was easier to build.

93
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:37,080
It was also several months ahead in development.

94
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:44,440
With a top speed of about 330 miles per hour, it was almost as fast as the Spitfire.

95
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:51,600
Spitfire designer Reginald Mitchell knew that he could and must do better,

96
00:07:51,601 --> 00:07:57,080
But his health was failing, and his fighter had not yet been accepted by the Royal Air Force.

97
00:07:57,540 --> 00:07:59,540
He drove himself even harder.

98
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:03,160
To have a machine that in your heart you knew was a winner,

99
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:07,180
and you knew that if war came, the whole nation would depend upon that machine,

100
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:10,840
I mean, it's one of those things that rarely happens to anyone.

101
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:13,540
And for someone to be aware of that,

102
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:16,540
and he must have been aware of it for quite a period before his death,

103
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:19,120
the mind boggles to think what a person is going through.

104
00:08:19,121 --> 00:08:26,120
Meanwhile, in Germany, the Luftwaffe, commanded by Hermann Goering,

105
00:08:26,121 --> 00:08:29,120
was growing bigger and stronger every day.

106
00:08:29,121 --> 00:08:36,120
By 1936, the German threat was obvious, even to Britain's air ministry.

107
00:08:36,121 --> 00:08:44,120
On June 3rd, an order was placed for 600 hurricanes, but only 310 Spitfires.

108
00:08:44,121 --> 00:08:49,120
Many still saw Mitchell's creation as a lightweight racing machine,

109
00:08:49,121 --> 00:08:53,120
fast, elegant, but not a fighter.

110
00:08:53,121 --> 00:09:00,120
As exhaustive testing continued, Mitchell finally lost his long fight against cancer.

111
00:09:00,121 --> 00:09:07,120
His death on June 11, 1937, devastated the Spitfire team.

112
00:09:07,121 --> 00:09:11,120
But the genius of his design still inspired them.

113
00:09:11,121 --> 00:09:16,120
Mitchell's deputy Joe Smith was able to keep the Spitfire dream alive,

114
00:09:16,121 --> 00:09:20,120
even though the work was a full year behind schedule.

115
00:09:20,121 --> 00:09:25,120
Britain's Air Ministry became increasingly frustrated and irritated by the delays,

116
00:09:25,121 --> 00:09:29,120
and there was talk of abandoning the Spitfire program altogether.

117
00:09:29,121 --> 00:09:36,120
Alex Henshaw was chief test pilot at Supermarine's new factory at Castle Bromwich.

118
00:09:36,121 --> 00:09:40,840
It was so advanced and so expensive that certainly in 1939,

119
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:42,720
both Geoffrey Quill and myself said,

120
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:45,320
oh, no, it's a marvelous aircraft,

121
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:46,860
but of course it'll never go into production.

122
00:09:47,440 --> 00:09:49,800
It's too difficult, too expensive.

123
00:09:50,100 --> 00:09:52,960
We had a contract for 300, and he said, Alex, you know,

124
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:54,560
he said, I reckon this will be the end of the Spitfire.

125
00:09:57,280 --> 00:09:59,420
As the German threat increased,

126
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:02,080
Britain's fighter production was increased.

127
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:04,480
But time was running out.

128
00:10:04,481 --> 00:10:07,480
And, of course, the dreadful thing to people like myself

129
00:10:07,481 --> 00:10:11,480
was the knowledge that I had and a lot of other people had

130
00:10:11,481 --> 00:10:13,480
that had travelled Germany,

131
00:10:13,481 --> 00:10:16,480
that they're a very, very efficient fighting machine,

132
00:10:16,481 --> 00:10:19,480
and that we were twiddling our thumbs, wasting time.

133
00:10:19,481 --> 00:10:22,480
And, my God, did we waste those months.

134
00:10:22,481 --> 00:10:26,480
In 1938, Britain and France had come very close to war with Germany

135
00:10:26,481 --> 00:10:29,480
over German demands on Czechoslovakia.

136
00:10:29,481 --> 00:10:33,480
But in the case of Britain, defence was really in an appalling state.

137
00:10:33,481 --> 00:10:38,480
Although aircraft production was being stepped up, it was still a trickle.

138
00:10:38,481 --> 00:10:44,480
Without this invaluable year, the RAF would probably have gone to war with biplanes.

139
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:53,480
On August 4th, 1938, the first operational Spitfire was finally delivered to an RAF station.

140
00:10:53,481 --> 00:11:01,480
19 Squadron North of London became the first fighter unit to exchange its biplanes for the new Spitfires.

141
00:11:01,481 --> 00:11:05,480
But the arms race was now on.

142
00:11:05,481 --> 00:11:08,480
Germany's Luftwaffe was already re-equipping

143
00:11:08,481 --> 00:11:11,480
with its answer to the Spitfire,

144
00:11:11,481 --> 00:11:14,480
the Messerschmitt Me 109.

145
00:11:14,481 --> 00:11:18,480
In 1939, Alex Henshaw was invited to Germany

146
00:11:18,481 --> 00:11:21,480
to see the new fighters.

147
00:11:21,481 --> 00:11:26,480
The Germans were very conscious of their military power,

148
00:11:26,481 --> 00:11:30,480
and I think also very conscious that we're not a very...

149
00:11:30,481 --> 00:11:33,380
a very competent lot anyway.

150
00:11:33,900 --> 00:11:37,520
In fact, when Major Sideman showed me the 109s,

151
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:39,460
I said, well, we have the Spitfire.

152
00:11:39,461 --> 00:11:43,700
He said, oh, yes, he said, very pretty little toy,

153
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:45,620
but the 109 is a fighting machine.

154
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:56,220
On September 3rd, 1939,

155
00:11:56,860 --> 00:11:59,340
the war began between Britain and Germany.

156
00:11:59,341 --> 00:12:04,340
Now, the RAF would have to face the Luftwaffe.

157
00:12:04,380 --> 00:12:08,340
Young British pilots were rushed through their basic training.

158
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:11,260
Most ended up flying hurricanes,

159
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:15,260
but the fortunate few were assigned to Spitfire squadrons.

160
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:28,260
One day, a Spitfire land on the airfield texted over to our hangar.

161
00:12:28,261 --> 00:12:33,260
We all rushed over to this thing, walked around it, stroked it, sat in it.

162
00:12:33,261 --> 00:12:37,260
I know I fell a bit in love with this aeroplane. It was beautiful.

163
00:12:37,261 --> 00:12:42,260
Like if you see a ship that looks as if it should sail.

164
00:12:42,261 --> 00:12:45,260
The Spitfire looked as if it should fly.

165
00:12:49,260 --> 00:12:52,260
The Spitfire as an aircraft was a thoroughbred.

166
00:12:53,260 --> 00:12:57,260
I think that with any aeroplane, if it looks right, it will fly right.

167
00:12:58,260 --> 00:13:02,980
and let's face it, it's pretty far the most beautiful aeropoe that's ever been made.

168
00:13:13,540 --> 00:13:20,100
The first time we flew it, we got into it, and it started up, oh, it was fantastic.

169
00:13:28,260 --> 00:13:40,180
You were so excited and so young, which was probably very relevant, that it was just a

170
00:13:40,181 --> 00:13:43,820
thrilling experience with lots of frights.

171
00:13:43,821 --> 00:13:52,400
You scared yourself many, many times because it reacted so differently from the old I-Blades.

172
00:13:52,401 --> 00:13:57,900
You didn't get into the aircraft and struck yourself in.

173
00:13:58,500 --> 00:14:00,520
You struck the aircraft to you.

174
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:02,140
You became part of the aircraft.

175
00:14:03,300 --> 00:14:05,380
My shoulders touched both sides of the cockpit

176
00:14:05,381 --> 00:14:08,820
and my head was about a quarter inch from the roof at the top.

177
00:14:09,620 --> 00:14:11,620
So you were part of the aeroplane.

178
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:13,080
That was a beauty of it.

179
00:14:16,440 --> 00:14:19,780
I think if Mitchell hadn't designed the Spitfire,

180
00:14:20,280 --> 00:14:21,680
we'd have been very pushed.

181
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:24,300
He was a brilliant man.

182
00:14:25,340 --> 00:14:29,260
Who could visualize something as beautifully shaped as this?

183
00:14:30,260 --> 00:14:33,640
It didn't look as if it was meant to be a fighting machine, did it?

184
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:37,100
It just looked as if it was a beautiful thing to fly, which it was.

185
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:40,320
And yet it could be a lethal weapon.

186
00:14:42,700 --> 00:14:48,220
Mitchell's pretty little toy was about to teach the German pilots a new game.

187
00:14:48,221 --> 00:14:57,420
On October 16, 1939, German aircraft first attacked Britain by attempting to bomb the fourth bridge at Edinburgh.

188
00:14:57,980 --> 00:15:00,960
The raid was said to be an exercise for the German Luftwaffe.

189
00:15:01,520 --> 00:15:05,180
No damage was done, although three German aircraft were shot down.

190
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:08,060
The Spitfire will continue in a moment.

191
00:15:13,660 --> 00:15:17,980
In May 1940, the Germans invaded France.

192
00:15:18,220 --> 00:15:32,220
Within a month, France had fallen.

193
00:15:33,300 --> 00:15:34,600
Britain now stood alone.

194
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:44,780
Churchill told his country the Battle of Britain is about to begin.

195
00:15:44,781 --> 00:15:49,460
By July 1940, Britain's position seemed really bleak.

196
00:15:50,180 --> 00:15:52,340
It had always been assumed that German bombers

197
00:15:52,341 --> 00:15:56,120
would have to fly all the way from their bases in Germany to Britain,

198
00:15:56,500 --> 00:15:59,220
and the distance was too great for fighters to accompany them.

199
00:15:59,980 --> 00:16:01,620
Now some bombers were based

200
00:16:01,621 --> 00:16:05,320
scarcely more than 20 miles from the shores of Great Britain,

201
00:16:05,780 --> 00:16:08,780
and fighters could accompany them all the way to their targets.

202
00:16:08,781 --> 00:16:19,820
the battle began with dive bombing attacks on shipping in the english channel as germany

203
00:16:19,821 --> 00:16:28,640
prepared for an invasion of england all that stood between britain's survival and defeat

204
00:16:28,641 --> 00:16:35,480
was a narrow strip of sea and young fighter command pilots like 19 year old dave glaser

205
00:16:35,481 --> 00:16:39,820
As far as I was concerned, we were there to stop it.

206
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:45,480
If there was an invasion, we'd have a damn good go at the enemy.

207
00:16:46,620 --> 00:16:51,300
Soon, Britain's chain of coastal radar stations came under attack.

208
00:16:54,720 --> 00:16:57,300
Then, the fighter airfields.

209
00:16:57,301 --> 00:17:08,400
Without proper training, pilots like Bam Bamberger relied on their aircraft and on their courage.

210
00:17:08,900 --> 00:17:14,940
I think really survival at that stage depended more on luck than anything else.

211
00:17:15,700 --> 00:17:18,440
Bob Doe needed all the luck he could get.

212
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:22,280
No one told us anything before we went to action.

213
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:24,980
In fact, I'd only ever fired by guns once.

214
00:17:24,981 --> 00:17:27,260
we were given, I think, 20 rounds per gun.

215
00:17:27,700 --> 00:17:29,340
We were told to go and shoot them to the North Sea.

216
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:31,800
Well, you can't miss the North Sea, really.

217
00:17:35,520 --> 00:17:37,200
We got our Spitfires in March,

218
00:17:37,201 --> 00:17:38,920
and we flew them every day

219
00:17:38,921 --> 00:17:41,260
until we were shoved into the Battle of Bretton.

220
00:17:47,500 --> 00:17:51,040
That summer produced the best summer I think we'd had for ages.

221
00:17:52,020 --> 00:17:53,280
During that short period,

222
00:17:53,281 --> 00:17:55,520
I only remember one day when we couldn't fly.

223
00:17:57,080 --> 00:18:00,920
Good flying weather meant daily visits by the Luftwaffe.

224
00:18:01,740 --> 00:18:04,460
Losses began to cause serious concern.

225
00:18:05,100 --> 00:18:08,580
By mid-July, it appeared that if such losses continued,

226
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:12,640
the RAF would be eliminated in six weeks.

227
00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:17,380
As the pilots waited to take off,

228
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:21,360
they knew how slim their chances of survival were.

229
00:18:23,280 --> 00:18:34,220
Waiting for the scramble was much worse, in my opinion, than flying in combat.

230
00:18:34,640 --> 00:18:44,420
I was very tense, probably apprehensive of what I was going to come up against.

231
00:18:44,640 --> 00:18:46,080
It was all an unknown quantity.

232
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:49,320
Okay, you try to read.

233
00:18:50,260 --> 00:18:53,260
Mostly, I think, you try to sleep or doze.

234
00:18:53,280 --> 00:18:54,880
but it was waiting.

235
00:18:55,660 --> 00:19:00,040
We used to play cards or painting, drawing.

236
00:19:01,380 --> 00:19:02,680
A lot of them did sleeping.

237
00:19:03,760 --> 00:19:06,420
And then suddenly the telephone would ring.

238
00:19:07,560 --> 00:19:09,300
And you'd be all alert.

239
00:19:10,260 --> 00:19:14,400
And it was someone saying the nappy was coming around with tea.

240
00:19:14,401 --> 00:19:24,840
The RAF was outnumbered roughly two to one.

241
00:19:25,240 --> 00:19:29,840
To even the score, it had to use its limited resources effectively.

242
00:19:30,560 --> 00:19:31,980
The key was radar.

243
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:38,300
This pioneering form of electronic warfare was primitive, but it worked.

244
00:19:38,301 --> 00:19:44,980
With a skilled operator, the range, bearing, altitude, and scale of an attack could be predicted,

245
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:48,200
giving defensive fighters a head start.

246
00:19:48,660 --> 00:19:54,840
The radar stations reported to Fighter Command's headquarters at Bentley Priory, northwest of London.

247
00:19:55,260 --> 00:20:02,180
From here, the order to engage the enemy would pass through the sector controllers to individual fighter squadrons.

248
00:20:02,181 --> 00:20:03,020
6-2.

249
00:20:04,520 --> 00:20:05,980
AP-78.

250
00:20:08,300 --> 00:20:12,460
The chain reaction would begin.

251
00:20:13,180 --> 00:20:14,780
A blip on the radar screen.

252
00:20:15,580 --> 00:20:17,240
A telephone call to headquarters.

253
00:20:19,940 --> 00:20:25,500
We have 40 plus bandits at angles 1-5, vector 1-8.

254
00:20:25,501 --> 00:20:27,480
A rapid assessment of the situation.

255
00:20:28,380 --> 00:20:31,640
A decision on which squadrons should intercept the enemy.

256
00:20:32,980 --> 00:20:35,660
A final phone call and then the order.

257
00:20:36,420 --> 00:20:37,720
Squadron, scramble.

258
00:20:38,300 --> 00:20:48,100
It was almost with relief when the scramble came.

259
00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:53,380
The phone would ring and we'd leap up from whatever we were doing

260
00:20:53,381 --> 00:20:55,680
and start running towards our aeroplanes.

261
00:20:57,700 --> 00:21:06,460
You were so preoccupied that at that point you hadn't time to feel apprehensive or scared.

262
00:21:06,461 --> 00:21:10,460
Everything now depended on speed.

263
00:21:10,461 --> 00:21:13,460
The squadron had to get airborne in a matter of minutes

264
00:21:13,461 --> 00:21:15,460
and gain height as quickly as possible

265
00:21:15,461 --> 00:21:18,460
to engage the enemy on equal terms.

266
00:21:22,460 --> 00:21:27,460
Every pilot's greatest fear was getting caught on the ground,

267
00:21:27,461 --> 00:21:30,460
a helpless victim of a surprise attack.

268
00:22:05,240 --> 00:22:06,440
So, let's go.

269
00:22:35,240 --> 00:22:36,440
Let's hope.

270
00:22:36,460 --> 00:22:50,460
Now, the young pilots of Mitchell Spitfires would have to fight for their lives.

271
00:22:56,700 --> 00:23:00,060
In June 1940, Herman Goering declared,

272
00:23:00,220 --> 00:23:03,560
My Luftwaffe is invincible, and now we turn to England.

273
00:23:03,940 --> 00:23:06,340
How long will this one last? Two, three weeks?

274
00:23:06,460 --> 00:23:09,060
The Spitfire will return in a moment.

275
00:23:14,120 --> 00:23:19,040
Fighter pilot, the words convey something of the spirit that lies in our squadrons.

276
00:23:19,640 --> 00:23:22,740
Something young and vigorous, decisive, wanting action at once,

277
00:23:23,140 --> 00:23:27,480
and finding its expression in the struggle going on daily over our southeastern shores.

278
00:23:32,620 --> 00:23:35,920
But you have not yet been let into the mind of the men who do the flying,

279
00:23:35,921 --> 00:23:39,300
nor shown their feelings when in the air and going into a fight.

280
00:23:39,700 --> 00:23:40,800
I was very frightened.

281
00:23:42,320 --> 00:23:45,680
I suppose it was... I suppose it was natural to be frightened.

282
00:23:46,200 --> 00:23:49,580
And we did everything wrong we could possibly do wrong.

283
00:23:50,060 --> 00:23:53,660
We flew off in close formation, which is the most stupid thing you can do,

284
00:23:54,380 --> 00:23:58,360
which means the only person not formating is the leader,

285
00:23:58,580 --> 00:24:00,140
so there's only one person looking around.

286
00:24:00,620 --> 00:24:03,940
The leader looks round to see if the rest of his section are with him.

287
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:09,880
One is lagging a bit and he's told briskly on the radio telephone to close up.

288
00:24:10,400 --> 00:24:16,100
I was probably not concentrating so much on flying a good formation.

289
00:24:16,340 --> 00:24:17,920
I was looking for those enemy fighters.

290
00:24:18,600 --> 00:24:20,540
And then they climb up to patrol height.

291
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:26,660
We got up the same height we were told the enemy were,

292
00:24:26,780 --> 00:24:28,980
which again is fairly stupid, you want to be above them.

293
00:24:29,320 --> 00:24:32,600
We proceed to patrol up and down some, which is idiotic,

294
00:24:33,020 --> 00:24:35,620
because half the time you can't see a blind thing behind you.

295
00:24:35,920 --> 00:24:40,820
The majority of pilots who were shot down during the Battle of Britain

296
00:24:40,821 --> 00:24:44,500
were shot down from behind and taken by surprise.

297
00:24:45,160 --> 00:24:47,960
So that's why you developed a rubber neck.

298
00:24:48,780 --> 00:24:51,480
You could turn your head through 360 degrees.

299
00:24:52,200 --> 00:24:54,120
Suddenly the leader sights something.

300
00:24:55,660 --> 00:24:57,960
Carry ho. There they are, jenny's.

301
00:24:58,240 --> 00:25:02,820
First time I saw 100-plus German aircraft, I knew we were going to attack it.

302
00:25:02,821 --> 00:25:05,420
I thought, well, this must really be it.

303
00:25:05,920 --> 00:25:09,920
We just found ourselves in the middle of hundreds of aircraft.

304
00:25:10,920 --> 00:25:12,520
And that's when the battle started.

305
00:25:13,240 --> 00:25:18,820
You had to keep a jolly good watch out for the right position to bring the boys round and then in.

306
00:25:25,360 --> 00:25:32,120
The natural instinct for these bombers in close formation was to break with this spit coming down.

307
00:25:32,121 --> 00:25:36,120
When you're flying an airplane, you're moving as well as the target.

308
00:25:36,121 --> 00:25:40,120
You see, you have to compute in your head how far in front of that airplane you've got to fire,

309
00:25:40,121 --> 00:25:46,120
because when you fire these bullets, it takes a little time for it to reach, shall we say, 300 yards.

310
00:25:46,121 --> 00:25:49,120
And in that time, an airplane can move a long way.

311
00:25:51,120 --> 00:25:55,120
The slower-moving bombers presented fairly easy targets,

312
00:25:55,121 --> 00:26:00,120
but any attack on them instantly provoked retribution from above

313
00:26:00,121 --> 00:26:03,120
as fighter escorts swept in to attack.

314
00:26:03,121 --> 00:26:07,120
The important thing is to see the other person before they see you.

315
00:26:07,121 --> 00:26:11,120
If you see him first, you've got a damn good chance of shooting him down,

316
00:26:11,121 --> 00:26:14,120
or alternatively avoiding him.

317
00:26:14,121 --> 00:26:17,120
But if you don't see him, that's when you're in real trouble.

318
00:26:17,121 --> 00:26:22,120
And suddenly, these 109s flash past with their yellow noses.

319
00:26:22,121 --> 00:26:28,120
They look killers and they look professional and I felt very much an amateur.

320
00:26:29,120 --> 00:26:38,120
I saw a tracer going past my aircraft and someone in the squadron shouted break, which I did.

321
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:45,120
And suddenly I found myself alone in the sky and there was no one else there, not even any enemy aircraft.

322
00:26:45,121 --> 00:26:52,120
I found myself behind a 11-0, so I closed up and shot at it.

323
00:26:52,121 --> 00:26:57,120
And I was utterly astonished when this team turned over and dived down into the sea.

324
00:26:57,121 --> 00:27:00,120
I then followed him down.

325
00:27:00,121 --> 00:27:06,120
I saw him crash into the sea, and as I pulled up, I realised I was being shot at from behind.

326
00:27:06,121 --> 00:27:10,120
And this aircraft overshot me.

327
00:27:10,121 --> 00:27:11,320
after he overshot me.

328
00:27:13,360 --> 00:27:16,280
So I settled behind him and shot him down.

329
00:27:16,281 --> 00:27:19,480
So I shot two down in my first operation

330
00:27:19,481 --> 00:27:21,900
without knowing what was happening in the sky around me

331
00:27:21,901 --> 00:27:23,960
or who was doing what.

332
00:27:23,961 --> 00:27:26,340
Everyone was looking after themselves

333
00:27:26,341 --> 00:27:28,600
or trying to achieve something.

334
00:27:28,601 --> 00:27:33,600
And so it was individuals taking flash shots

335
00:27:35,280 --> 00:27:36,680
at other individuals.

336
00:27:36,681 --> 00:27:43,680
For many young pilots, their first mission was also their last.

337
00:27:43,681 --> 00:27:49,680
In my case, I was sure luck that I wasn't killed in my first action.

338
00:27:49,681 --> 00:27:51,680
It wasn't any skill on my part.

339
00:27:51,681 --> 00:27:57,680
My aircraft was not hit, and I didn't fire my guns,

340
00:27:57,681 --> 00:28:01,680
but I came back to base slightly wiser.

341
00:28:01,681 --> 00:28:08,960
Until you've learned you haven't the hope of living,

342
00:28:09,700 --> 00:28:12,080
unless you're very, very lucky, as I was.

343
00:28:13,700 --> 00:28:16,580
Each aircraft had its own ground crew.

344
00:28:17,240 --> 00:28:20,420
The crew's job was to prepare it as quickly as possible

345
00:28:20,421 --> 00:28:21,680
for the next engagement.

346
00:28:22,540 --> 00:28:25,940
All eight machine guns had to be checked and reloaded.

347
00:28:26,440 --> 00:28:30,320
Each gun was fed by a belt of 300 rounds of ammunition,

348
00:28:30,321 --> 00:28:32,460
2,400 rounds total.

349
00:28:33,060 --> 00:28:36,820
The pilot had roughly 15 seconds worth of firepower.

350
00:28:37,640 --> 00:28:40,040
Meanwhile, the fuel tanks were refilled,

351
00:28:40,380 --> 00:28:42,240
the oxygen supply was replaced,

352
00:28:42,540 --> 00:28:46,500
and any engine problems reported by the pilot were investigated.

353
00:28:47,460 --> 00:28:53,160
The pilot and his crew knew that his life depended on their skills.

354
00:28:53,720 --> 00:28:56,220
A close bond often grew between them.

355
00:28:58,240 --> 00:29:00,220
The ground crew had their own airplane.

356
00:29:00,320 --> 00:29:01,820
which they looked after.

357
00:29:01,821 --> 00:29:03,080
And I think they took more interest

358
00:29:03,081 --> 00:29:05,320
in what happened than anyone did.

359
00:29:05,321 --> 00:29:07,060
And they're questioning you all the time,

360
00:29:07,061 --> 00:29:09,260
they're getting you out of the plane.

361
00:29:09,261 --> 00:29:11,700
They're very enthusiastic, they're wonderful.

362
00:29:15,960 --> 00:29:18,000
They were in it just as much as we were,

363
00:29:18,001 --> 00:29:19,140
there's no doubt about it.

364
00:29:19,141 --> 00:29:22,140
They had a hell of a job to do.

365
00:29:23,780 --> 00:29:26,240
Despite the bitterness of the struggle,

366
00:29:26,241 --> 00:29:29,240
German and RAF fighter pilots often displayed

367
00:29:29,241 --> 00:29:32,000
a strong sense of chivalry in combat.

368
00:29:34,140 --> 00:29:37,420
I couldn't bring myself to shoot at him anymore.

369
00:29:37,421 --> 00:29:40,580
And so I flew up alongside him about 20 yards away.

370
00:29:41,620 --> 00:29:45,220
I could see this tall, blonde, big bloke

371
00:29:45,221 --> 00:29:46,660
just sitting there looking at me.

372
00:29:47,860 --> 00:29:50,700
So I just put my hand up and turned away and left him.

373
00:29:50,701 --> 00:29:53,560
I knew he'd had to go down to the sea.

374
00:29:53,561 --> 00:29:55,560
And in fact, it turned out I should have shot him down

375
00:29:55,561 --> 00:29:57,900
because he was picked up on his own side

376
00:29:57,901 --> 00:30:00,380
and taken back and he came out and shot more of our people down.

377
00:30:00,940 --> 00:30:03,980
So I should have shot them down, but I just couldn't bring myself to do it.

378
00:30:05,900 --> 00:30:09,240
The 109 started to smoke very early on and I stopped firing,

379
00:30:09,820 --> 00:30:11,060
partly to save ammunition.

380
00:30:11,980 --> 00:30:13,900
And partly, I suppose, that I sort of, uh,

381
00:30:15,200 --> 00:30:16,700
not really wanting to kill somebody.

382
00:30:17,420 --> 00:30:18,960
So I flew up alongside,

383
00:30:19,780 --> 00:30:23,900
slid my cockpit canopy back and could see him struggling with this 109

384
00:30:23,901 --> 00:30:26,140
to try and rescue the airplane and himself.

385
00:30:26,141 --> 00:30:29,140
Unfortunately, about 500 feet, he lost control.

386
00:30:29,141 --> 00:30:33,140
I went straight in and burst into flames.

387
00:30:37,140 --> 00:30:42,140
I never once considered I was shooting at a person.

388
00:30:42,141 --> 00:30:44,140
They were just aircraft I was shooting at.

389
00:30:44,141 --> 00:30:48,140
It never entered my head of a pilot being in it.

390
00:30:50,140 --> 00:30:54,140
Soon, wrecked aircraft littered the English countryside.

391
00:30:54,141 --> 00:30:57,140
This was an entirely new kind of warfare,

392
00:30:57,141 --> 00:31:01,140
fought in full view of the civilian population,

393
00:31:01,141 --> 00:31:03,140
but far above their heads.

394
00:31:03,141 --> 00:31:07,140
In the gardens and the harvest fields of southern England,

395
00:31:07,141 --> 00:31:12,140
the people gazed up at the drama unfolding above them.

396
00:31:12,141 --> 00:31:14,140
Already in the First World War,

397
00:31:14,141 --> 00:31:17,140
fighter pilots had become popular heroes.

398
00:31:17,141 --> 00:31:22,140
And now in 1940, the fighter pilots of fighter command,

399
00:31:22,141 --> 00:31:25,740
with their casual clothing and their devil-may-care attitude,

400
00:31:25,780 --> 00:31:29,940
became gladiators, acting out this drama in the skies

401
00:31:29,980 --> 00:31:31,820
over South-East England,

402
00:31:31,860 --> 00:31:35,220
with the population like an audience looking up at them.

403
00:31:35,221 --> 00:31:49,221
them.

404
00:31:50,460 --> 00:32:03,980
The mission of the Luftwaffe pilots was to destroy the RAF and its bases at all cost.

405
00:32:03,981 --> 00:32:14,020
But on September 7, 1940, there was a sudden and dramatic change of plan.

406
00:32:14,021 --> 00:32:20,480
Hitler ordered a massive bombing campaign against London.

407
00:32:20,481 --> 00:32:23,740
The Blitz had begun.

408
00:32:23,741 --> 00:32:27,240
For Londoners, it was the beginning of a nightmare.

409
00:32:27,241 --> 00:32:32,480
But for the hard-pressed RAF, it was a reprieve.

410
00:32:32,481 --> 00:32:41,260
In 1940, during Operation Dynamo, roughly 350,000 British and Allied troops were evacuated from Dunkirk in the face of the German Luftwaffe.

411
00:32:41,800 --> 00:32:44,140
The Spitfire will continue in a moment.

412
00:32:49,300 --> 00:32:56,580
As the Luftwaffe switched to bombing London, the Battle of Britain entered a new and far more bitter phase.

413
00:32:56,581 --> 00:32:59,740
This was no longer a gladiatorial contest.

414
00:33:00,420 --> 00:33:03,080
This was the slaughter of innocent civilians.

415
00:33:03,660 --> 00:33:05,940
But while Londoners paid the price,

416
00:33:06,500 --> 00:33:09,280
the RAF got the breathing space it needed

417
00:33:09,281 --> 00:33:12,800
to rebuild its strength and avenge the victims.

418
00:33:15,440 --> 00:33:19,340
There's absolutely no doubt that the German change of emphasis

419
00:33:19,341 --> 00:33:22,560
from bombing radar stations and attacking the RAF

420
00:33:22,561 --> 00:33:25,420
to bombing London was a major mistake.

421
00:33:26,140 --> 00:33:29,840
Events later in the war were going to show that neither the German bombing of England

422
00:33:29,841 --> 00:33:33,000
nor the Allied bombing of Germany, which was much, much heavier,

423
00:33:33,360 --> 00:33:35,840
would actually break civilian morale.

424
00:33:36,700 --> 00:33:38,880
So by taking the pressure off the RAF,

425
00:33:39,280 --> 00:33:42,780
the Germans actually made the decisive mistake in the Battle of Britain.

426
00:33:45,060 --> 00:33:47,420
As British productivity improved,

427
00:33:47,780 --> 00:33:51,360
replacement Spitfires began coming off the production lines

428
00:33:51,361 --> 00:33:53,620
faster than the Germans could shoot them down.

429
00:33:53,621 --> 00:33:57,680
They left the factory as anonymous and identical machines.

430
00:33:58,140 --> 00:33:59,800
But once in the hands of a pilot,

431
00:34:00,280 --> 00:34:05,220
each one began to acquire an identity and a personality.

432
00:34:07,260 --> 00:34:10,320
You became acquainted with your own aircraft.

433
00:34:11,200 --> 00:34:16,040
And your own aircraft had a particular smell, a particular feel.

434
00:34:16,720 --> 00:34:19,920
From the time I got my first aeroplane in the squadron,

435
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:21,780
I always tried to fly D for dough.

436
00:34:21,781 --> 00:34:25,280
and whilst you can't fly it on every occasion

437
00:34:25,281 --> 00:34:28,440
because it does have to be repaired or serviced on occasions

438
00:34:28,441 --> 00:34:32,720
any other time, any other aeroplane never felt quite right

439
00:34:32,721 --> 00:34:35,520
whereas D for Doe did, it was mine

440
00:34:35,521 --> 00:34:39,720
My favourite aircraft had G on it

441
00:34:39,721 --> 00:34:45,560
and if G wasn't serviceable for some reason

442
00:34:45,561 --> 00:34:47,560
I was at very bad temper

443
00:34:47,561 --> 00:34:52,400
The one I... when I bailed out and this thing went into the sea,

444
00:34:52,401 --> 00:34:57,000
I was flying another chap's aeroplane because mine was in for maintenance.

445
00:34:57,001 --> 00:34:59,440
And he was furious.

446
00:35:01,440 --> 00:35:07,200
Both the Spitfire and the Hurricane took a surprising amount of punishment, quite happily.

447
00:35:07,201 --> 00:35:12,000
I mean, on one occasion, I had bullets straight through my main spar on the Spitfire.

448
00:35:12,001 --> 00:35:15,280
It really flew back quite happily, no problem.

449
00:35:15,281 --> 00:35:18,760
And I think almost every time I went into action, I got hit.

450
00:35:19,320 --> 00:35:20,820
I never knew where from.

451
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:23,680
On one occasion, I had 12 bullets in the thing.

452
00:35:24,280 --> 00:35:26,040
Very lucky, none of them hit me, of course.

453
00:35:26,880 --> 00:35:30,340
It made you think, this is a dangerous business I'm in.

454
00:35:31,160 --> 00:35:33,000
Like most Spitfire pilots,

455
00:35:33,200 --> 00:35:38,040
Dennis Robinson was hit from behind by a German fighter he never even saw.

456
00:35:39,080 --> 00:35:43,080
The only thing I could think to do, and I did it accidentally, spontaneously,

457
00:35:43,081 --> 00:35:45,320
It just pushed the control column hard forward.

458
00:35:46,600 --> 00:35:49,120
And the port-wheel Spitfire actually stood on its nose.

459
00:35:49,220 --> 00:35:50,220
I was absolutely amazed.

460
00:35:54,420 --> 00:35:56,220
Down I went, absolutely vertical.

461
00:36:02,580 --> 00:36:05,760
I managed to pull out in time in a steep climbing turn,

462
00:36:06,340 --> 00:36:08,780
hoping to see my adversary in full power.

463
00:36:09,280 --> 00:36:11,600
And in the middle of that, the engine stopped dead on me.

464
00:36:13,080 --> 00:36:18,120
Fortunately, there was another aircraft in the sky.

465
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:20,760
It often happens in battle, you know,

466
00:36:20,820 --> 00:36:22,460
that you suddenly find yourself all alone.

467
00:36:24,700 --> 00:36:27,420
I had a pretty good touchdown with the wheels up.

468
00:36:28,320 --> 00:36:30,200
I was feeling pretty pleased with myself,

469
00:36:30,340 --> 00:36:33,320
and all of a sudden, the copper was filled with dust and dirt,

470
00:36:33,400 --> 00:36:35,560
and then I could feel the Spitfire lifting up again,

471
00:36:35,640 --> 00:36:37,800
and I thought I was crashing over on my back.

472
00:36:37,840 --> 00:36:39,040
I don't really know what happened,

473
00:36:39,500 --> 00:36:41,380
except the left wing was torn right off.

474
00:36:41,381 --> 00:36:46,900
As soon as the action stopped, of course, I immediately threw the canopy back

475
00:36:46,901 --> 00:36:52,420
and realized then that the Spitfire finished up vertically in a ditch and I could easily jump clear.

476
00:36:53,420 --> 00:36:58,960
Even a Spitfire as badly damaged as this one could help keep others in the air.

477
00:36:59,740 --> 00:37:07,860
Reusable wings, engines and tails were simply grafted onto new or repaired fuselages to produce more fighters.

478
00:37:07,861 --> 00:37:13,620
But while the aircraft could be patched up, exhausted pilots could not.

479
00:37:22,200 --> 00:37:28,240
The overriding memory that I have of the Battle of Ridden period is a terrible tiredness.

480
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:34,280
We were on duty from half an hour before first light until half an hour after dusk.

481
00:37:34,281 --> 00:37:38,120
I know that if I sat down and went to sleep during that period,

482
00:37:38,121 --> 00:37:41,280
I don't know whether it's lack of sleep or nerves.

483
00:37:41,281 --> 00:37:44,280
I think it's probably a combination of the two.

484
00:37:44,281 --> 00:37:46,280
Your nerves are working like mad.

485
00:37:52,280 --> 00:37:57,280
The most flights I did involved in Jordan Battleboro was five,

486
00:37:57,281 --> 00:37:59,280
five scrambles in one day.

487
00:37:59,281 --> 00:38:13,281
day and at the end of a day like that you are both physically and mentally

488
00:38:13,400 --> 00:38:18,320
stressed out you know you'd had it

489
00:38:21,840 --> 00:38:25,640
I think it was difficult to deal with because you had to deal with your own

490
00:38:25,641 --> 00:38:29,400
anxiety about what was going to happen, which way it would happen to you.

491
00:38:29,480 --> 00:38:35,240
I think we settled for the fact that there wasn't much chance of surviving, really.

492
00:38:36,520 --> 00:38:41,680
Among the pilots, there was grim determination to finish the job,

493
00:38:41,920 --> 00:38:45,100
but no one knew how much longer they could hold out.

494
00:38:49,240 --> 00:38:52,140
As the Battle of Britain reached its climax,

495
00:38:52,740 --> 00:38:55,300
raids on London became more intense.

496
00:38:55,640 --> 00:39:06,900
Large areas of the city were destroyed as huge fires raged out of control.

497
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:12,720
The turning point came on September 15, 1940,

498
00:39:13,180 --> 00:39:17,440
as the first reports began streaming in from the coastal radar stations.

499
00:39:17,441 --> 00:39:22,520
The empty map at Fighter Command Headquarters began filling with evidence

500
00:39:22,521 --> 00:39:25,760
that this day's raid would be the biggest yet.

501
00:39:32,760 --> 00:39:35,740
500 German raiders headed for London.

502
00:39:36,060 --> 00:39:40,100
They were intercepted by more than 300 spitfires and hurricanes.

503
00:39:52,520 --> 00:40:06,520
As Londoners watched, a spectacle unfolded over the city.

504
00:40:12,100 --> 00:40:17,940
The sky was full of dogfighting as the German formations broke up and scattered.

505
00:40:17,941 --> 00:40:23,940
At the end of the day, the RAF proudly claimed 185 kills.

506
00:40:26,940 --> 00:40:29,660
Later, revised down to 60.

507
00:40:30,300 --> 00:40:31,320
But it was enough.

508
00:40:32,740 --> 00:40:36,780
Two days later, Hitler abandoned his invasion plans.

509
00:40:37,800 --> 00:40:40,080
The Battle of Britain was over.

510
00:40:41,540 --> 00:40:46,760
We did very well over the Battle of Britain, no doubting that at all.

511
00:40:46,761 --> 00:40:52,020
but God was on our side because, as was said at the Battle of Waterloo,

512
00:40:52,600 --> 00:40:55,020
it was a very, very close-run thing.

513
00:40:56,880 --> 00:40:59,040
The Germans hadn't won air superiority

514
00:40:59,041 --> 00:41:02,400
and without it they had no chance of invading across the Channel.

515
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:05,220
And they hadn't hurt Britain so badly

516
00:41:05,221 --> 00:41:09,000
that the British government or the British population wanted to sue for peace.

517
00:41:09,680 --> 00:41:12,020
Britain was still there, still in the war.

518
00:41:12,021 --> 00:41:16,440
An unsinkable aircraft carrier for Allied bombing attacks on Germany.

519
00:41:16,760 --> 00:41:22,100
and that springboard from which the Allies would eventually mount the invasion of Europe.

520
00:41:23,180 --> 00:41:25,680
It was all down to the Battle of Britain.

521
00:41:26,160 --> 00:41:29,840
And there was a great tribute to the fighter which had helped make it all possible.

522
00:41:30,520 --> 00:41:35,460
The German air-ace Adolf Gallen was asked by Goering what he really wanted to win the battle.

523
00:41:35,461 --> 00:41:38,080
He replied, Spitfires.

524
00:41:38,081 --> 00:41:52,081
the spitfire had steadily improved in stages throughout six long years of war production of

525
00:41:53,360 --> 00:41:59,920
the aircraft britain's ministry once proposed to cancel eventually reached more than 22 000

526
00:42:01,680 --> 00:42:07,040
i think if mitchell could have lived and seen this beautiful machine do all the things it did

527
00:42:08,080 --> 00:42:13,120
I think he would have been very proud, as he should have been, too.

528
00:42:13,600 --> 00:42:23,060
I've flown Defiance, I've flown Lysanders, I've flown jet aircraft, I've even flown helicopters.

529
00:42:24,500 --> 00:42:30,100
And the only aircraft I really loved was the Spitfire.

530
00:42:32,140 --> 00:42:37,760
The Spitfire captured the public imagination like no other before or since.

531
00:42:38,080 --> 00:42:40,280
It became a symbol of freedom,

532
00:42:41,180 --> 00:42:43,980
which helped to save Britain and the world

533
00:42:43,981 --> 00:42:46,540
from what Winston Churchill called

534
00:42:46,541 --> 00:42:50,320
the New Dark Age of Nazi tyranny.

535
00:42:56,020 --> 00:43:00,540
This was Churchill's final verdict on the young RAF airmen

536
00:43:00,541 --> 00:43:02,420
who fought the Battle of Britain.

537
00:43:03,520 --> 00:43:06,400
Never in the field of human conflict

538
00:43:06,401 --> 00:43:11,400
has so much been owed by so many to so few.

